\p{\strong{Status:} draft. This is the full text of the design document, as written on 2026-11-24, moved into the journal the same day with its wording and its section numbers unchanged. What it describes is a design with a prototype on a private copy of the machine; nothing of it is in the main tree. The short note on it is [the value model, second version](design-values-v2), and [the decision](dec-36MX) says in which form the change may enter the machine.}

\p{Author: agent U2, 2026-11-24. Status: DESIGN + PROTOTYPE on a private copy (\code{\verb~|work/u2/tree/rlvm-machine~}, the main tree of \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} as copied under the sandbox lock at 19:04:34Z); nothing of it is in the main tree. It revises \code{\verb~|ISA.md~} section 2.5 (hash-consing discipline) and fills the last row of the table of ISA section 10 ("non-interned cells ... v2 experiment"). Authorities, in this order: \code{\verb~|OWNER-NOTES.md~} (notes 3, 5, 6: the JIT is for the checking process; 8: storage strategies; 11: official verdicts; 12: warm wall clock with 8 workers), \code{\verb~|RECONCILIATION.md~}, \code{\verb~|ISA.md~}. Evidence it starts from: [rep-truffle-T3](rep-truffle-T3) sections 5 and 7 (the tier has nothing to remove because every value is interned at once), [rep-X1](rep-X1) (census of every record), \code{\verb~|P8.md~} 4.6 (2.6 times the leader's instructions per step), [rep-machine-M1](rep-machine-M1) (the store), \code{\verb~|review/soundness-machine.md~}. Living summary and raw data: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-U2.md~}, \code{\verb~|work/u2/~}.}

\p{Numbers in this document are exact counts of one run unless a table says "warm wall clock"; every timing was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window (section 10 says which).}

\subtree{
\title{0. The answer in one page}

\p{\strong{The model in ten lines.}}

\ol{
\li{A ref denotes one value for the life of a session, and equal refs are equal values. That is all a verdict needs.}
\li{Canonical refs ("equal values have equal refs", I-ID) are SHARING, and sharing is needed in three places only: the subject of a memoised judgment (class M: WHNF, CORE, UNF, TYPE and the PROOF / PROPT flags of an application; VAL of a suspension), the components of a key (class K: frame slots = tabling and closure identity, arguments of an interned application, PROJ / NSUC / FVAR), and the two sides of a comparison (class C: the pair table).}
\li{Atoms, frames, closures, suspensions and tabling records keep their identity from birth: they are keys by purpose and most of their probes hit.}
\li{An APPLICATION built at a lazy site is born PRIVATE: record and spine are written into the ordinary slabs, one bit says "in no table"; no hash, no probe. Lazy site = \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} of a code block, except the application that the next instruction reduces (a memo key that hits four times in five).}
\li{It is IDENTIFIED - arguments first, iteratively; one probe; entered in place under its own ref, or made a forwarding duplicate of the record that already has the key - at the moment it enters class K, M or C. Reading, matching, projecting, passing, returning, storing it as a memo value do not identify it.}
\li{Invariant: every component of an identified record or interned run is identified. So I-ID holds for identified values exactly as ISA 2.5 states it, and with the three classes complete the machine makes the judgments it makes today, tick for tick (the completeness test: ticks with the mode on = ticks with the mode off).}
\li{A probe that is not made is worth a cache miss where a session has outgrown the cache and less than its bookkeeping where it has not: lazy sites are lazy only in sessions of 2^17 records and more, and a site whose applications are identified anyway learns to intern at birth (adaptive sites).}
\li{Nothing is reclaimed before the session reset; a private record is a record (its footprint counts).}
\li{To a JIT a private application is a reserved ref plus a virtual object, written only where the ref leaves the compilation unit; both tiers give every record the same ref; tier equivalence compares identified records.}
\li{Soundness: nothing answers "different" from unequal refs; a forward is set only after the whole key compared equal against a table member; no ref is reused. Gates inside the mode are word for word as today.}
}

\p{\strong{Sizing (census, before building).} Block instructions make 70 \% of reachability's records and \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} alone 47 \%; half of its executions find an existing record (T3). Applications are 24-59 \% of the records and are found again 3.5-21 \% of the time; closures and delayed arguments hit two times in three (X1). A per-activation or per-judgment nursery has nothing to release (the hot blocks return what they build). So: applications, lazily, until they are a key.}

\p{\strong{What the prototype measured} (private tree, one binary, \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.u2=N~}; 10.1, 10.2). Applications never identified = probes not made: reachability 3.04 M per run (63 \% of the mode-off records, 36 \% of all record-table probes), Cedar slice 0.29 M (6 \%), Init 1.95 M (12 \%), Mathlib slice 1.17 M (13 \%), Std 2.90 M (10 \%), at -0.1 to +1.6 \% ticks and +4 to +36 \% records (block sites, every session). One session per declaration: ticks within 0.02-0.2 \% of today's. Warm wall clock, JVM, \code{\verb~|WarmMain~}: reachability -13 \% at one worker and -8 \% at eight (block sites); with the large-session trigger and adaptive sites \strong{-16.7 \% at one worker (888 against 1066 ms, ranges disjoint) and -10.0 \% at eight (460 against 511 ms)}; the Cedar slice +4 \% at one worker when every session is lazy (not resolved), +9 \% with every site lazy (resolved), and unchanged - counters identical to the mode-off run - with the trigger. Native image: reachability -11.9 \% at one worker (1185 against 1345 ms, ranges disjoint), \strong{no gain at eight workers} (+3 \%, inside the scatter; workers' CPU -1 \%); Cedar slice unchanged with the trigger.}

\p{\strong{Gates.} M8's full gate (differential per declaration against the reference engine on tutorial, bugs, the 230 tests, Init, Std, reachability, the Cedar slice; tier, convention and audit lanes; the real driver) with every site lazy (mode 47), with block sites (43) and with the first form (11): PASS WITH DECLINES each, SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, 0 differences in every lane, the declines being the tree's eager-reduction declines. The hostile suite of the soundness review with mode 47 on every machine: 32 switch sets x 329 exports, 0 unexpected verdicts. Own hostile cases: \code{\verb~|U2ValuesTest~}, 6 tests. The recommended mode (235) passes the same gate; with it and with the mode off the module's default test group is green (451 tests).}

\p{\strong{Recommendation.} Adopt a restricted form in stages (section 11): stage 0 the plumbing (no behaviour change); stage 1 the staging switch \code{\verb~|lazyid~} = lazy \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} of blocks in large sessions, classes K / M / C, adaptive sites - OFF by default until the eight-worker gain is resolved on the platform that ships (JVM: -10 \%, ranges touching; native image: none); stage 2 complete classes M and C until the completeness test reads +0.000 \%, then widen; stage 3 the tier ("reserve, do not write", with T3's CALL). Keep the present model for closures, suspensions and frames.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What identity is used for today}

\p{Today (ISA 2.5, I-ID) every record is entered into \code{\verb~|vtab~} (and every FRAME / REST run into \code{\verb~|rtab~}) in the operation that makes it: a hash over its key, a load of a table slot that is cold once a session has outgrown the caches (NOTES-M1 5.3: four fifths of a probe on reachability), a comparison on a hit, a table store and later one more probe at the session reset on a miss. Identity then serves the following mechanisms. The last two columns say what each of them needs from a value: nothing but "equal refs denote equal values" (the direction that hash-consing gives for free and that a ref to ANY record gives), or the full I-ID "equal values have equal refs" (canonical ids).}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|#~}\th{Mechanism}\th{Keyed by}\th{A hit gives}\th{Needs canonical ids for a right verdict?}\th{What non-canonical ids cost}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{\code{\verb~|CONV~} c0, \code{\verb~|a == b~} (the official \code{\verb~|quick_is_def_eq~} / \code{\verb~|is_eqp~})}\td{the two refs}\td{T without work}\td{no: unequal refs fall through to the complete procedure, which answers T on equal values (section 8.1)}\td{the structural test \code{\verb~|Same~} (D28, bounded), or the full comparison of two equal values}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{pair table POS / NEG / FAILED (the official \code{\verb~|m_success~} and failure caches)}\td{the unordered pair of refs}\td{T / F without work}\td{no: in plain mode the official answer is a function of the pair, so a recomputation gives it again; under eager reduction the machine declines (S-F5)}\td{a comparison done again}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{memo words of an application / constant / projection: \code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~}, flags \code{\verb~|PROOF~}, \code{\verb~|PROPT~}, \code{\verb~|COREFULL~}}\td{the record}\td{the result of a reduction or inference}\td{no: a memo word of any record is a fact about the value that record denotes}\td{\strong{the reduction done again for every duplicate}: this is where gruszka's ground machine loses a factor of 10 (R05 2.11) and where mode 3 below loses 26 \% ticks on reachability}}
\tr{\td{4}\td{\code{\verb~|VAL~} / \code{\verb~|TYPE~} / \code{\verb~|TSTAMP~} of a suspension = tabling of \code{\verb~|EVAL~}, \code{\verb~|INFER~}, \code{\verb~|CHECK~}}\td{\code{\verb~|(term, PRUNE(frame), ls)~}: the frame's slots are refs}\td{the value / type of a term under bindings}\td{no}\td{a term evaluated again for every spelling of its bindings; a shared node of a DAG is the exponential case}}
\tr{\td{5}\td{closure identity \code{\verb~|(term, frame, ls)~} and its memo words \code{\verb~|DOM~}, \code{\verb~|COD~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~} (PII), \code{\verb~|SLVL~}, \code{\verb~|CODE~}}\td{as 4}\td{the same closure for the same bindings}\td{no}\td{as 4}}
\tr{\td{6}\td{free variables \code{\verb~|FVAR(type, k)~}, \code{\verb~|FV0~}, the scope protocol (ISA 2.8)}\td{the ref of the type}\td{the canonical variable of a binder}\td{no (two spellings of one type give two families of variables; I-FV2 still holds), but see 8.1 item 6}\td{memo hits under binders are lost}}
\tr{\td{7}\td{direct-mapped caches: \code{\verb~|CHEAD~}, sorts, \code{\verb~|IC~}, \code{\verb~|LSUB~}, \code{\verb~|SIGT~}, \code{\verb~|UNDEC~}, closed-term memo, APPLY memo, REST chain}\td{refs or (ref, site)}\td{one probe saved}\td{no (every cell is a hint or carries its full key)}\td{misses}}
\tr{\td{8}\td{the OPEN bit table, the record's flag bits}\td{the record}\td{-}\td{no (functions of the value, I-FLAG)}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{9}\td{warm sessions}\td{records of earlier declarations}\td{everything above across declarations}\td{no}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{10}\td{tier and convention equivalence in the gates (NOTES-M8)}\td{record words, run words, pairs, ticks}\td{"two executions did the same"}\td{not a verdict; the comparison is word for word today}\td{a new named mode needs its own definition (section 7)}}
}

\p{Reading: \strong{no verdict needs canonical ids; every use of them is sharing} - which is not a small thing: rows 3, 4 and 5 are what make the machine terminate in seconds on proof terms that are DAGs. The question is therefore not "which values may lose identity" but "for which values is the probe paid before it is known that anything will ever look them up", and the census answers it: applications (section 9).}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The model}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 Three kinds of values}

\table{
\tr{\th{Kind of value}\th{Identity}\th{Why}}
\tr{\td{immediates (Nat below 2^31), \code{\verb~|NONE~}, \code{\verb~|HOLE~}}\td{the ref is the value}\td{no record}}
\tr{\td{atoms: SORT, CONST, FVAR, NBIG, STR}\td{canonical at birth (through \code{\verb~|CHEAD~}, the sort cache, \code{\verb~|FV0~}, the literal import)}\td{a handful per declaration, found by a direct-mapped cache; FVAR is identity by definition}}
\tr{\td{keys by purpose: the tabling record of \code{\verb~|EVAL~} / \code{\verb~|INFER~} / \code{\verb~|CHECK~} (plain SUSP), frames, PROJ and NSUC cells}\td{canonical at birth}\td{they exist to be looked up; \code{\verb~|gated~}, \code{\verb~|bodies~} and X1's \code{\verb~|once~} decide WHETHER one is made, not this document}}
\tr{\td{\strong{applications (NEU)}}\td{\strong{private at birth at a lazy site, identified on demand}}\td{19-59 \% of the records, found again 3-20 \% of the time (X1 C2, P2); built by the hundred thousand as constructor arguments, types and delayed redexes that nothing looks up}}
\tr{\td{closures and delayed arguments (LAM, PI, SUSP made by \code{\verb~|MKSUSP~}) and their frames}\td{canonical at birth in the prototype; stage D of section 11 (design only)}\td{two thirds of their probes hit (X1: LAM 63-66 \%, delayed arguments 62 \%): the opposite regime, see 9.3}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 Life cycle of an application}

\pre{\verb~|
            mkAppLazy                      ident: key absent                 (session reset)
  (nothing) ---------> PRIVATE ------------------------------> IDENTIFIED ----------------> gone
                          |                                        ^
                          |   ident: key present                   |  forward (word c)
                          +---------------------------> DUPLICATE -+
~}

\ul{
\li{\strong{PRIVATE}: record and spine are written in the ordinary slabs (\code{\verb~|vh~}, \code{\verb~|rs~}); no hash is computed, no table is touched; word c is 0; one bit in \code{\verb~|Values.Ext.privBits~} says "in no table". Every reader works on it unchanged: kind, flags, head, spine, memo words, \code{\verb~|FIELDS~}, \code{\verb~|SWITCH~}, \code{\verb~|PEEK~}, \code{\verb~|Same~}, \code{\verb~|Quote~}, \code{\verb~|Show~}.}
\li{\strong{IDENTIFIED}: the record is in \code{\verb~|vtab~} under its key; all its arguments are identified (2.3 V2). An application made by an interning constructor is born here, as today. A private one gets here IN PLACE: same record, same ref.}
\li{\strong{DUPLICATE}: when a private application is identified and its key is already in the table, the record stays what it is (a valid value for whoever holds its ref) and word c holds the ref of the canonical record; the operation that asked stores the canonical ref. A duplicate is never in a table and never becomes a component of a key.}
\li{There is no fourth state "shared / published": values never leave a session (what workers share is terms, fact records and code objects). "Published" is "identified".}
\li{Nothing is reclaimed before the session reset (section 4.4 says why not yet).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 Invariants}

\ul{
\li{\strong{V1 (a ref means one value)}: for the life of a session a ref denotes one value. The identity words of a record are written once, with two exceptions that keep the value: an argument slot of a private application may be replaced by the canonical ref of the same value, and word c of an application goes 0 -> its hash (identified) or 0 -> the canonical ref (duplicate).}
\li{\strong{V2 (keys are canonical)}: every component of an identified record or of an interned run is identified: arguments of an identified application, slots of a FRAME / REST run, the struct of a PROJ, the base of an NSUC, the type of an FVAR. Hence for identified values I-ID of ISA 2.5 holds as it is written: two identified refs are equal iff they are the same construction.}
\li{\strong{V3 (one direction for everybody)}: equal refs denote equal values (V1). Unequal refs prove nothing unless both are identified. No judgment may answer "different" from unequal refs (none does today: 8.1).}
\li{\strong{V4 (flags and memo words)}: the flags of a private application are those of its interned twin (computed by the same rule from head and arguments); a memo word of any record is a fact about its value and may be used, but it is shared only between holders of that record. A memo that must be shared is read through the canonical record (2.4, class M).}
\li{\strong{V5 (tables)}: a record is in \code{\verb~|vtab~} iff its private bit is clear; growth and reset of \code{\verb~|vtab~} skip set bits; \code{\verb~|Hash.ofRecord~} is called for table members only (word c of a private record is not a hash).}
\li{\strong{V6 (determinism)}: which records are made, which are private, when each is identified and every ref are functions of the sequence of operations: nothing depends on time, on the host depth bound or on the worker. Convention equivalence (NOTES-M8) therefore holds word for word inside the mode.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 What forces identity, and why each is necessary}

\p{\code{\verb~|Values.ident(v)~}: \code{\verb~|v~} unless \code{\verb~|v~} is a private application; a duplicate: its canonical ref; else: identify the arguments (deep, iteratively - a private nest is as deep as a list is long), probe the key; absent: enter the record itself; present: make it a duplicate. Cost: one probe per private record reached, i.e. what was not paid at birth.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Class}\th{Operation}\th{What is identified}\th{Necessary because}\th{If it were omitted}}
\tr{\td{K (keys)}\td{an interning constructor of an application (\code{\verb~|mkAppKey~}, \code{\verb~|mkNeu~}; extension of an application)}\td{every argument (and the copied prefix)}\td{V2: the key of an identified record}\td{no wrong verdict; the application has a second id per spelling of its arguments, and everything keyed on it (rows 3-5 of section 1) misses}}
\tr{\td{K}\td{\code{\verb~|PRUNE~}, \code{\verb~|FRAME~}, the REST chain, \code{\verb~|closureAt~} (closures, delayed arguments, tabling records)}\td{every binding the frame keeps}\td{V2: frames are the keys of tabling and of closure identity}\td{tabling and closure hits become spelling-dependent: unbounded re-evaluation on shared terms}}
\tr{\td{K}\td{\code{\verb~|MKPROJ~} that stays a PROJ cell, \code{\verb~|NSUCC~} that makes an NSUC cell}\td{the struct, the base}\td{V2}\td{as the first row}}
\tr{\td{K}\td{\code{\verb~|OPEN(type)~}, \code{\verb~|mkFVar~}}\td{the type}\td{V2, and row 6 of section 1: one family of variables per type}\td{memo hits under binders are lost; two closures over the "same" binder type open different variables}}
\tr{\td{M (memo keys)}\td{\code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|DELTA~} of a private application whose memo word is empty, and \code{\verb~|VTYPE~} of a private application}\td{the application}\td{the memo (\code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~}) is the hit that makes repeated reduction and inference free; it belongs to the canonical record}\td{\strong{measured: +25.6 \% ticks on reachability with lazy code blocks, +143 \% with every site lazy} (modes 3 and 7 of section 10.1, WHNF alone); without CORE / DELTA / VTYPE: +1.8 \% ticks on Init where WHNF alone was identified, +1.1 \% with them}}
\tr{\td{M}\td{(design; not in the prototype) the two flag memos: \code{\verb~|ISPROP~} (\code{\verb~|PROPT~}) and \code{\verb~|PIRR~} (\code{\verb~|PROOF~}) of a private application}\td{the application}\td{the flags are memo words too}\td{the type of a duplicate is asked "is it a proposition" again: the visible residue of the completeness test with every site lazy (10.1: \code{\verb~|ISPROP~} +226 k on Init)}}
\tr{\td{C (comparison)}\td{\code{\verb~|CONV(a, b)~} after the test \code{\verb~|a == b~} (prototype: bit 32)}\td{both sides (design, not in the prototype: also the argument pairs that \code{\verb~|CARGS~} and the lazy-delta step compare by ref without entering \code{\verb~|CONV~})}\td{row 2 of section 1: the pair table is keyed by refs; and it restores \code{\verb~|a == b~} for equal values}\td{\strong{measured: +38 \% ticks on the Cedar slice with every site lazy} (mode 15 against 47); nothing measurable when only code blocks are lazy}}
\tr{\td{-}\td{a site that is known to make a memo key: \code{\verb~|KCONST ; MKAPP d ; WHNF _, d~} (the scrutinee of a case tree)}\td{the application, at its birth (it is not a lazy site)}\td{T3's count for U1: 464,637 such applications per pass on reachability, 92,000 distinct, four of five answered by the WHNF memo}\td{a private record made and thrown away as a duplicate four times out of five}}
}

\p{What does NOT force identity: reading (\code{\verb~|FIELDS~}, \code{\verb~|SWITCH~}, \code{\verb~|BKIND~}, the projection of a constructor application, \code{\verb~|PEEK~}), passing (arguments of \code{\verb~|APPLY~} / \code{\verb~|CALL~} / \code{\verb~|TAILAPPLY~}, results, registers, environment slots, scratch), storing as a memo VALUE (\code{\verb~|VAL~}, the result word of \code{\verb~|WHNF~} / \code{\verb~|UNF~} / \code{\verb~|CORE~}, an inline-cache value), extension (the prefix is copied), the structural test \code{\verb~|Same~}, readback. A private application that is only built, passed, matched and dropped never costs a probe: that is the whole gain.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Lazy sites: which constructions are born private}

\p{A lazy site calls \code{\verb~|Values.mkAppLazy~}; every other caller of \code{\verb~|Values.mkApp~} interns as today. The mode bits (system property \code{\verb~|rlvm.u2~}, the named mode is \code{\verb~|lazyid~}; 0 = ISA 2.5 as it is):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Bit}\th{Name}\th{Meaning}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{DATA}\td{a lazy site builds an application whose head is weak-head normal (constructor, inductive type, axiom, free variable: flag WHNF) privately}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{REDEX}\td{... and an application whose head may reduce (flag ACTIVE: definition, theorem, recursor, quotient, projection, Nat function), except the scrutinee of the next \code{\verb~|WHNF~}}}
\tr{\td{4}\td{ALL}\td{every \code{\verb~|Values.mkApp~} is a lazy site (the generic \code{\verb~|EVAL~} walk, the rules, string expansion); without it only \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} of code blocks is}}
\tr{\td{8}\td{MEMO}\td{class M of 2.4: \code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|DELTA~}, \code{\verb~|VTYPE~} identify a private application before they compute}}
\tr{\td{32}\td{CONV}\td{class C of 2.4}}
\tr{\td{64}\td{ADAPT}\td{adaptive sites (below)}}
\tr{\td{128}\td{BIG}\td{lazy only in a large session (below)}}
\tr{\td{16}\td{STAT}\td{counters (process-wide, printed at exit)}}
}

\p{The modes that were measured: \strong{1} (data built by blocks), \strong{11} = 1 + 2 + 8 (everything blocks build, memo shared), \strong{43} = 11 + 32, \strong{47} = 43 + 4 (every site), \strong{107} / \strong{111} = 43 / 47 with adaptive sites, \strong{171} / \strong{235} = 43 / 107 in large sessions only, and the deliberately broken ones, \strong{3} and \strong{7} / \strong{15} (redexes without class M, every site without class C), which exist to show what classes M and C are worth.}

\p{\strong{Adaptive sites (bit 64).} Whether a site's applications are ever identified is a property of the site, and it can be learned - the owner's "use whatever specialization / JIT trick": an inline cache of the site's demand for identity. The prototype's site is the constant at the head (its declaration row, direct-mapped into 2^13 pairs of saturating byte counters per machine: made / identified; both are halved when one saturates). A private record of an adaptive site carries its site in word c (\code{\verb~|-(site + 1)~}: negative = private with a site, 0 = private, positive = forward) and reports to its site when it is identified. A site with at least 32 samples whose records were identified three times in four interns at birth again, except for one construction in 16, which stays lazy as a sample (so a site can come back). The decision changes which records are private, never a value; it is a function of the machine's history, so V6 holds per machine and per schedule, not across worker counts (as warm sessions already make ticks depend on which declarations a session saw). A site per block instruction (the code index of the \code{\verb~|MKAPP~}) and per term node of the generic walk would be sharper; not built.}

\p{\strong{Lazy only in a large session (bit 128).} A probe that is not made is worth a cache miss where the tables have outgrown the cache and next to nothing where they have not - and in a small session the bit tests of the key-forming loops and the duplicate records are all that is left (10.2: the Cedar slice at one worker, +2 to +4 \%). With this bit a lazy site interns as ever until the session holds 2^17 records (\code{\verb~|rlvm.u2.big~}; 4 MiB of records, a \code{\verb~|vtab~} of 1 MiB); from then on it is lazy. A warm session is reset at a footprint of 1 MiB, so only a single declaration that is itself large gets there: the mode is inert on library corpora (the Cedar slice: 0.01 M private applications, every counter the mode-off value) and keeps 88 \% of the probes it avoids on reachability (2.67 M of 3.04 M never identified), whose four giant declarations make 93 \% of its records (X1 C8). This is the session-level storage strategy: the store changes its discipline when its contents no longer fit the representation that was cheap for them.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Representation}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 In the stepper (as built)}

\pre{\verb~|
vh[8r .. 8r+7]   w0 = NEU | flags | n     a = head     b = spine run     c = 0, or -(site + 1), or the forward (> 0)
                 m0 .. m3 = memo words: its own until it is identified (class M identifies before they are used)
rs[run ..]       header (SPINE, n, OPEN) | 0 | a1 .. an        a spine never was in rtab
privBits         bit r = 1: record r is in no table (private, or a forwarding duplicate)
registers, environment slots, scratch, spines of private records, memo VALUES:   the ref r, as for any value
~}

\p{There is no young region and no second kind of ref: a private application lives where every record lives, and what distinguishes it is one bit that only the key-forming loops, the memoised judgments and \code{\verb~|CONV~} read.}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Machine.addRecordFree(w0, a, b, c)~}: the bump of \code{\verb~|addRecordAt~} without the table store; \code{\verb~|Machine.enterRecordAt~} enters an existing record; \code{\verb~|Machine.vtabUsed~} replaces \code{\verb~|vp - vbase~} in the load test (a private record does not occupy a slot), in the reset heuristics and in the footprint.}
\li{\code{\verb~|Values.neuPriv~}: the miss branch of \code{\verb~|neu~} / \code{\verb~|neuExt~} (flags inherited from the head or the extended application, OPEN = or over the arguments through the OPEN bit table, spine written by a loop) without hash and probe.}
\li{\code{\verb~|Values.Ext.privBits~}: one bit per record (1/256 of the record slab), cleared at the reset in O(used / 64); \code{\verb~|privMade~} (private records of this session) is the one word every key-forming loop tests: 0 = the loops are the loops of today.}
\li{Forwarding: word c of the duplicate (positive). No side table. Word c of a private record is 0, or negative: the adaptive site it reports to.}
\li{\code{\verb~|Values.ident~} / \code{\verb~|identSlow~}: explicit stack of (record, next argument) pairs in \code{\verb~|Values.Ext.istk~}; no host recursion (test: a nest 300,000 deep).}
\li{Force points (2.4): \code{\verb~|Values.neu~}, \code{\verb~|neuExt~}, \code{\verb~|closureAt~}, \code{\verb~|mkProj~}, \code{\verb~|nsuccSlow~}, \code{\verb~|mkFVar~}; \code{\verb~|Runs.pruneNear~}, \code{\verb~|internRun~}; \code{\verb~|Scope.open~}; \code{\verb~|Reduce.whnf~}, \code{\verb~|core~}, \code{\verb~|delta~}, \code{\verb~|Infer.vtype~} (bit 8); \code{\verb~|Conv.conv~} (bit 32). A forced binding is written back into the environment slot / scratch word it came from, so the hash and the comparison that follow see canonical refs and a second capture of the same slot costs one bit test.}
\li{Lazy site: \code{\verb~|Code.mkApp~} (with the scrutinee test of 2.4).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 What was considered instead}

\table{
\tr{\th{Alternative}\th{Why not}}
\tr{\td{ref bit 30 \code{\verb~|REF_EPH~} (ISA 2.1, reserved for exactly this) with a nursery slab of its own}\td{27,000 lines read \code{\verb~|vh[v << 3]~}; every one would need the test. The bit table gives the same information to the few places that need it (the key-forming loops) and costs the readers nothing}}
\tr{\td{a young intern table in front of \code{\verb~|vtab~} (M1's "young set")}\td{keeps I-ID, removes the cold load only if hits are recent; X1 C7: 56-87 \% of the hits on the Cedar slice and reachability come 2,048 ticks or more after the record was made, and M1's front caches lost time (NOTES-M1 5.3)}}
\tr{\td{region rollback per judgment (X1 b1)}\td{releasing costs a probe per record, needs an undo log at every memo store; X1: no-go as a speed-up}}
\tr{\td{Reussir-style reuse bits (X1 a, c)}\td{no-go by census (reuse cases 0.4-7 \%, the source always referenced)}}
\tr{\td{one global "is this value shared" bit maintained at every copy of a ref (\code{\verb~|REF_UNQ~})}\td{ISA section 10: one missed mark is an unsound write; nothing here needs to know uniqueness, only "is in the table"}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 Storage strategies (owner note 8)}

\p{The model leaves the room the note asks for and uses the first instance of it: "private application" IS a storage strategy in the GraalJS sense - a value keeps a cheaper representation (no table entry, no hash) while what is done with it allows it, and transitions to the general one (identified) when an operation needs it; the transition is \code{\verb~|ident~}, it is one-way, and the strategy tag is the bit of \code{\verb~|privBits~}. Packed lists / arrays / strings (kinds 13-15) would enter the same way: born packed and private, unpacked by the constructor view, identified by \code{\verb~|ident~} (which then needs a canonical key for the packed form - not designed here).}

\p{\strong{Reussir's bits, once more.} X1 found that the one-bit "unique" of Reussir has nothing to bite on in a store where every cell is shared by definition. Private applications are the first population of this machine that is NOT shared by definition - so the question comes back in a narrower form: could a private application whose only holder is the instruction that just took it apart (\code{\verb~|FIELDS~} after \code{\verb~|SWITCH~}) be rewritten in place for the next construction? In the stepper: no - "only holder" is not known (registers, environment slots and memo words hold refs without counting them), and what would be saved is a bump of ten words, not a probe. In compiled code the question answers itself: a virtual object that does not escape is never written at all (section 5), which is reuse with the compiler's ownership analysis instead of bits. That is where the owner's idea lands in this design.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 Why nothing is reclaimed}

\p{A private application that was never identified is garbage at the latest when no register, environment slot, memo word or private record holds its ref - which nobody tracks (ISA section 10: no counts). The prototype keeps it until the session reset, like every record. Consequences: the footprint of a session GROWS in this mode (the duplicates that interning would have folded: section 10.1, records +23 to +44 \% on reachability), the machine's own session cap is reached earlier, and a giant declaration needs more memory, not less. A nursery that is released at the return of the activation that made it would need the escape information of 5 and is the memory half of stage E.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. What a private value is to a JIT}

\p{Owner notes 5 and 6: the JIT compiles the checking process, i.e. blocks. NOTES-T3 section 7: a block builds values and asks judgments; compiled code must repeat every probe, so escape analysis has no object. With lazy sites the block-built applications are no longer global side effects at birth - but the stepper still WRITES them (record and spine in the slab), and a write into a shared array is an escape for the compiler too. Three steps make them removable, in this order:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Reserve, do not write.} The id of a private application and its spine are fixed by two bumps (\code{\verb~|vp~}, \code{\verb~|rsp~}); the words can be written later. In a compiled block \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} at a lazy site reserves the id and keeps head and arguments in a virtual object (\code{\verb~|PApp(id, head, a1..an)~} in a frame slot); every instruction of the same compilation unit that READS it (\code{\verb~|FIELDS~}, \code{\verb~|SWITCH~}, \code{\verb~|BKIND~}, extension) reads the object's fields; \strong{materialisation} - writing the eight words and the spine at the reserved id - happens where the ref leaves the unit: an operand of a boundary call (a judgment, an interning constructor, \code{\verb~|ident~}), a store into a machine register that outlives the unit, the result. Escape analysis then removes object and write for every application that is consumed inside the unit. Because the id was reserved, \strong{both tiers give every record the same ref}, written or not, so everything keyed by refs (hashes, direct-mapped cells) behaves identically and the lossy caches cannot make ticks tier-dependent. The reservation itself is two additions, the private bit and one store (word 0 = kind 0, so that a walker of the slab - an audit, a digest, the session-end census - sees "no record" at a reserved id that was never written).}
\li{\strong{Calls (T3's U1).} \code{\verb~|CALL~} passes and returns refs as they are (T3, requests.log 19:32Z: "a private application can be an argument and a result with no rule on my side"). With direct call nodes the unit of step 1 grows to caller + inlined callee: the callee's \code{\verb~|PProd.mk a b c d~} returned to a caller that takes one field is never materialised - NOTES-T3 7.3's "arguments nobody inspects" become dead code. The scrutinee of a \code{\verb~|CALL~} that takes the generic path is a memo key and is interned at birth (2.4, last row; T3's finding).}
\li{\strong{A nursery (later).} Reserved-but-never-written ids cost address space, not memory traffic; where even that matters (giant sessions), the reservation can come from a per-activation range that is popped at the return if nothing was materialised. This needs "no ref of the range survives", which step 1's escape information gives for compiled code only.}
}

\p{The stepper keeps the simple form (write at birth): it is the reference semantics, and its write is the materialisation "at the first instruction".}

\p{\strong{Tier equivalence in this mode} (the rule T3 confirmed is enough for its nodes): two tiers are equivalent iff, unit by unit, they give the same outcome and reason, the same ticks and call events, the same pair entries, and \strong{the same identified records with the same refs and words, in the same order of identification}; a private record is compared only through what holds it: its ref (reserved identically in both tiers) and, once it is materialised in both, its words. A record that only one tier materialised is not a difference. \code{\verb~|M8Tiers~} (values by structural fingerprint) needs no change: a fingerprint follows arguments, private or not.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. How the rest of the machine works with it}

\table{
\tr{\th{Subject}\th{Rule}}
\tr{\td{session reset}\td{\code{\verb~|vtab~} is emptied by fill or by one probe per IDENTIFIED record (private ones are skipped: an absent entry would cost a scan of the whole table); \code{\verb~|privBits~} is cleared in O(used / 64) and falls back to its base size after a large session; \code{\verb~|vtabUsed = 0~}}}
\tr{\td{table growth}\td{\code{\verb~|growVtab~} re-enters identified records only, in id order (X1's argument for deletions still holds: a record identified in place is entered later than records with larger ids, which is harmless for open addressing without deletions)}}
\tr{\td{footprint, session cap}\td{\code{\verb~|engineBytes~} counts every record and run word (private or not) and the occupied table slots (\code{\verb~|vtabUsed~}); \code{\verb~|Values.Ext.bytes~} counts the bit table. A warm session therefore reaches the machine's 1 MiB cap earlier (resets: section 10.1)}}
\tr{\td{budgets, ticks}\td{identification is part of the operation that asks for it, as a probe is today: no tick, no call event, no frame; it cannot be suspended (no judgment runs inside it)}}
\tr{\td{tabling (\code{\verb~|gated~}, \code{\verb~|bodies~}; X1's \code{\verb~|once~})}\td{unchanged: the key is \code{\verb~|(term, PRUNE(frame), ls)~} and PRUNE identifies what it keeps (class K), so a tabling hit is exactly today's. \code{\verb~|once~} hashes the kept bindings before \code{\verb~|table~}: it must hash them AFTER they are identified (one call of \code{\verb~|Values.identKept~} in \code{\verb~|firstVisit~}), else the filter sees two spellings of one key and evaluates once more - a tick difference, never a value}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Same~} (D28, the official \code{\verb~|is_equiv~})}\td{structural by construction ("false is not seen, never different"): it walks private applications like any other. With lazy sites it is consulted for pairs that \code{\verb~|a == b~} answered before; its fuel (256 nodes, 40 levels) bounds what it sees}}
\tr{\td{pair table}\td{keyed by refs as they are; canonical under class C. An entry made for a private ref stays valid (V1)}}
\tr{\td{inline caches, \code{\verb~|CHEAD~}, closed-term memo, APPLY memo}\td{hold refs as values: a private ref is a valid value. Cells keyed by a ref miss for a duplicate}}
\tr{\td{free variables}\td{\code{\verb~|OPEN~} identifies the type first; the scope protocol is untouched}}
\tr{\td{literals}\td{not applications; \code{\verb~|Nat.succ x~} goes through \code{\verb~|nsucc~} as before (an NSUC cell identifies its base)}}
\tr{\td{readback, \code{\verb~|Show~}, debug audits}\td{read records: unchanged. An audit that enumerates \code{\verb~|vtab~} or expects every record in it must skip private bits (the gate's audit lanes are clean with the prototype: section 10.3)}}
\tr{\td{warm sessions}\td{a private record lives across declarations like any record (I-SESSION: every constant it mentions was visible when it was made) and may be identified by a later declaration}}
\tr{\td{eager reduction (S-F5 / F26)}\td{the decline rule is untouched}}
\tr{\td{the T2 tier}\td{must mirror the lazy site and the scrutinee test in its \code{\verb~|MKAPP~} node (NOTES-T3 9.5: the tier follows \code{\verb~|Code.java~} by hand), then step 1 of section 5}}
\tr{\td{strict mode}\td{\code{\verb~|lazyid~} is an optimisation that changes what is computed (records): off in strict mode like every staging switch}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Ticks, records, gates}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Ticks, and the completeness test of the force points.} With classes K, M and C complete, every identity-dependent mechanism of section 1 sees canonical refs: the key of a memo is canonical (K, M), both sides of a comparison are canonical and so are all their components (C, V2). The machine then makes exactly the judgments it makes today; only the probes of values that never reach a key, a memo or a comparison are not made. So \strong{the tick count with the mode on must EQUAL the tick count with the mode off} - a tick difference is a path that reads a memo word or compares without identifying (never a verdict difference: it is lost sharing). Two things blur this in a warm session and must be taken out of the test: the machine's session cap is reached earlier (more records), and a reset forgets every memo; and adaptive sites make the set of private records depend on the history. The test is therefore run with one session per declaration (\code{\verb~|default,-warm~}): 10.1 has the result and the residue of the prototype.}
\li{\strong{Records.} More, not fewer: every lazy construction that interning would have answered with an existing record is now a record of its own (a duplicate once identified, or never looked at again). What falls is the number of PROBES and of table entries.}
\li{\strong{Convention equivalence} (host depth 0 / 128 / unbounded: outcome, reason, ticks, call events, trace, every record word, run word and pair): unchanged, by V6. The prototype passes it (10.3).}
\li{\strong{Tier equivalence K0 against K1} (declaration outcomes; \code{\verb~|M8Tiers~} values): unchanged.}
\li{\strong{A new named mode needs its own gate}, defined as: (1) the differential gate per declaration against the reference engine with the mode on; (2) the same outcome, reason and ticks ... as the mode-off run is NOT required (records differ by design; ticks may differ by the residue above); required instead: \strong{the same outcome and reason per declaration as the mode-off run, and no decline that the mode-off run does not have}; (3) convention equivalence inside the mode, word for word; (4) the audit lanes inside the mode; (5) \code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~} with the mode on in every switch set; (6) \code{\verb~|U2ValuesTest~} (I-ID of identified values under random construction, capture, extension and growth; flags; deep nests; clean tables after a reset); (7) the completeness test, as a gate once classes M and C are complete (stage 2); (8) the unit tests that assert K0 = K1 BY REF (\code{\verb~|M3TierTest~}, \code{\verb~|M7TierTest~}, \code{\verb~|M7TierHookTest~}: decision 6 "both tiers give the same record") compare \code{\verb~|Values.ident~} of both sides in a mode whose sessions are lazy: a block's application equals the generic tier's after identification, not at birth (10.3). With the large-session trigger they pass unchanged, because a unit test's session never gets there.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Soundness}

\subtree{
\title{8.1 Two equal values look different (a private application and its twin, or two private twins)}

\ol{
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|CONV~}.} \code{\verb~|a == b~} fails; \code{\verb~|convFast~} finds no pair; \code{\verb~|Same~} (default set) sees them as one expression within its fuel and answers T - the official \code{\verb~|is_equiv~}. Beyond the fuel the complete procedure runs on two structurally equal values. It answers T: both sides take the same deterministic steps (the same head, the same hints, so lazy delta unfolds both or compares the arguments of both; the same \code{\verb~|WHNF~} results up to V3), every sub-comparison is again between structurally equal values, and the official procedure answers T on identical terms without looking (so T is the official answer). What can differ is cost: a comparison the official kernel answers by pointer could run into the unit's tick bound here and DECLINE (the reference engine then decides) - never a wrong verdict. \code{\verb~|perf/loop-unfolded-once~} is the known member of this family today, without this mode. Not seen in any gate run with the mode (10.3: the declines are those of the mode-off run).}
\li{\strong{Pair table.} A miss recomputes. Plain mode: the official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is a function of the pair (review 4.3), so the recomputation is the official answer. Eager mode: the machine never compares (decline rule). NEG and FAILED entries are facts about refs, hence about values (V1).}
\li{\strong{Memo words} (\code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~}, \code{\verb~|PROOF~}, \code{\verb~|PROPT~}): recomputed per record; each is a function of the value in plain mode. The K-rule memo that depended on the mode is S-F5's subject and stays under the decline rule.}
\li{\strong{Tabling, closure identity.} Never affected: frames keep identified bindings only (V2).}
\li{\strong{Flags.} Functions of the value, computed by the same code (\code{\verb~|U2ValuesTest.flagsOfPrivateApplicationsAreThoseOfInternedOnes~}).}
\li{\strong{Free variables.} The type is identified before \code{\verb~|FV0~} / \code{\verb~|mkFVar~}, so a binder type has one family of variables as today. (Were it not: two spellings of a type would open \code{\verb~|FVAR(T', 0)~} and \code{\verb~|FVAR(T'', 0)~}, different records; I-FV2 "no two open binders hold the same variable" would still hold, a comparison of two closures opens ONE variable for both sides, and only memo hits under binders would be lost. The prototype does not rely on this.)}
\li{\strong{Projections, iota, K, structure eta, Nat offsets, string expansion}: all read head and spine; none looks up an application by its ref.}
\li{\strong{Level comparison, literals}: not applications.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|Nat.succ~}, canonical forms}: \code{\verb~|mkAppLazy~} applies the \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~} rule before anything else, as \code{\verb~|mkApp~} does.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.2 Two different values look equal}

\p{Only four things could do that, and none exists:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{A ref reused while somebody holds it.} Nothing is reclaimed before the session reset; the reset clears the bit table together with the slabs; a ref kept across a reset reads kind 0 as today (poison mode).}
\li{\strong{A wrong forward.} Word c of a duplicate is set only after the probe compared the whole key - head, length, every argument - against a table member, with all arguments identified first; by V2 and induction over the structure the two records are the same construction. (\code{\verb~|U2ValuesTest.identifiedValuesHaveOneIdPerConstruction~}: 6,000 random constructions per seed, lazy, interned, extended, captured, across table growth: one canonical id per structure and one structure per canonical id.)}
\li{\strong{A wrong in-place rewrite.} The only rewrite replaces an argument by the canonical ref of the same value (the result of item 2 or the argument itself).}
\li{\strong{A stale hash.} Word c of a private record is 0 and is never read as a hash (V5). Had it been: a hash only chooses a probe sequence; a hit compares the key.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.3 Where the official outcome depends on sharing or caching (review 4.1-4.4)}

\table{
\tr{\th{Case}\th{With \code{\verb~|lazyid~}}}
\tr{\td{S-F5 / S-F4: the official success cache and WHNF caches under eager reduction}\td{the machine declines such declarations in every regime; unchanged}}
\tr{\td{S-F1 / S-F2: congruence where the official procedure is not a congruence}\td{removed from every switch set; nothing here adds a congruence: \code{\verb~|Same~} is structural identity of expressions, not convertibility of arguments}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|nontransitive-*~}}\td{no transitive closure is introduced: a forward is identity of construction, not convertibility}}
\tr{\td{canonical free variables}\td{types identified (8.1 item 6)}}
\tr{\td{the official \code{\verb~|is_equiv~} shortcut that ends loops (\code{\verb~|loop-unfolded-once~})}\td{\code{\verb~|Same~} within its fuel; beyond it a decline at the tick bound, as today}}
\tr{\td{warm sessions}\td{everything a session keeps is still declaration-independent; a private record is no exception}}
}
}
}

