\p{What the rows say:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdicts}: accepted and declined are the mode-off numbers in every row (the declines are the tree's own eager-reduction declines); the machine rejects nothing in these corpora.}
\li{\strong{Closed evaluation (reachability)}: with block sites lazy (mode 43) 3.04 M applications per run are never identified - 78 \% of what blocks build lazily and 63 \% of ALL records of the mode-off run - at +0.0 \% ticks. The price is 1.72 M more records (+36 \%): the constructions that interning would have answered with an existing record (T3's 48 \% "found") are records now, and 0.85 M of them are identified later after all. Adaptive sites (107) keep the 3.03 M and cut the wasted constructions from 0.85 M to 0.17 M.}
\li{\strong{Library corpora}: the population is smaller and more of it is identified. Never identified with block sites (43): 0.29 M on the Cedar slice (6 \% of its records), 1.95 M on Init (12 \%), 1.17 M on the Mathlib slice (13 \%), 2.90 M on Std (10 \%); 47-64 \% of the lazily built applications ARE identified there, mostly because INFER asks for their type (\code{\verb~|VTYPE~}, class M) or CONV compares them. With every site lazy and no adaptation (47) four of five private applications are identified (Cedar slice 86 \%, Init 82 \%): records +37 to +61 \%, ticks +1.6 to +5.9 \%: \strong{every-site laziness without adaptation is a loss in counts on library corpora.} With adaptive sites (111) the same sites cost +5 to +10 \% records and +0.3 to +1.1 \% ticks and still avoid 0.31 M / 2.33 M / 1.02 M / 3.33 M probes.}
\li{\strong{What classes M and C are worth} (first build, before the scrutinee rule; \code{\verb~|work/u2/counts-v1/~}): redexes lazy without class M (mode 3): +25.6 \% ticks on reachability; every site lazy without M (mode 7): +143 \% ticks, +222 \% records; every site lazy without class C (mode 15): +38 \% ticks and +174 \% pair entries on the Cedar slice. The scrutinee rule alone brings mode 3 from +25.6 \% to +0.4 \% on reachability: nearly every memo hit of a block-built redex is the hit of \code{\verb~|MKAPP ; WHNF~}.}
\li{\strong{Footprint}: bytes in use at the resets +30 \% on reachability (263 -> 341 MiB), +2 \% on the Cedar slice, +10 \% on Init (mode 43); session resets +0 to +4 \% (the 1 MiB cap is reached earlier).}
}

\p{\strong{The completeness test of section 7} (one session per declaration, \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,-warm~}; \code{\verb~|work/u2/counts-nowarm/~}; ticks against the mode-off run of the same configuration):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{mode 11 (K, M)}\th{mode 43 (K, M, C)}\th{mode 47 (every site)}\th{mode 111 (every site, adaptive)}\th{where the residue is (call events that differ most)}}
\tr{\td{reachability (9,440,509 ticks)}\td{+0.129 \%}\td{\strong{+0.023 \%} (+2,132)}\td{+0.420 \%}\td{+0.070 \%}\td{43: \code{\verb~|CARGS~} +851, \code{\verb~|LSTEP~} +628; 47: \code{\verb~|EVAL~} +24 k, \code{\verb~|PIRR~}, \code{\verb~|ISPROP~}}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice (10,466,918)}\td{+0.250 \%}\td{\strong{+0.060 \%} (+6,270)}\td{+1.413 \%}\td{+0.193 \%}\td{43: \code{\verb~|CARGS~} +2.4 k, \code{\verb~|LSTEP~} +1.2 k; 47: \code{\verb~|EVAL~} +74 k, \code{\verb~|PIRR~} +18 k, \code{\verb~|ISPROP~} +18 k}}
\tr{\td{Init (50,797,296)}\td{-}\td{\strong{+0.197 \%} (+99,831)}\td{+2.297 \%}\td{-}\td{43: \code{\verb~|CARGS~} +61 k, \code{\verb~|LSTEP~} +18 k; 47: \code{\verb~|EVAL~} +650 k, \code{\verb~|ISPROP~} +226 k}}
}

\p{Reading: with classes K, M (WHNF, CORE, DELTA, VTYPE) and C the block-site mode is within 0.02-0.2 \% of the tick count of today's machine - what is left is argument pairs that \code{\verb~|CARGS~} and the lazy-delta step compare by ref without going through \code{\verb~|CONV~}'s entry (class C is applied there only), and the two flag memos \code{\verb~|PROOF~} / \code{\verb~|PROPT~} (\code{\verb~|PIRR~}, \code{\verb~|ISPROP~}), which the prototype does not treat as memo keys; with every site lazy the flag memos are the visible residue (\code{\verb~|ISPROP~} re-inferred per duplicate type, each with its \code{\verb~|EVAL~}). They are the two rows "M (design)" of 2.4: a complete implementation has four more force points and a tick count equal to today's.}

\p{\strong{Session-end census} (counting mode; every record of the ended sessions; from the mode-111 runs - suspensions and closures are built as in the mode-off machine):}

